Title: Speech to Sign Language Translator

Goal: To translate spoken or written words into sign language gestures, enabling hearing individuals to communicate effectively with the deaf or hard-of-hearing community.

Problem Solved: Bridges the communication gap by converting speech or text into visual signs in real time.

⚙️ How It Works
Speech Recognition

The system listens to the user’s voice input using a microphone.

Recognized words appear in the “Recognized Speech” panel (e.g., Subbu, Shubh, Namaste).

Text Processing

Converts recognized speech into text.

Supports multi-language translation (Telugu, English, Hindi, etc.) before converting to sign language.

Sign Generation

Each word or letter is mapped to a corresponding sign image or animation.

The “Sign Display” section shows the visual gesture (like the hand sign in your screenshot).

Playback Controls

Users can Play, Pause, Restart, or Repeat the sign animation.

Adjustable speed ensures clarity for learners or viewers.

🛠️ Tools & Technologies
Programming Language: Python / C# (depending on implementation).

Libraries: SpeechRecognition, Google Translate API, OpenCV, Tkinter or PyQt for GUI.

Dataset: Sign language gesture images or animations (ASL/ISL alphabets).

Hardware: Microphone and display device.

🌟 Key Features
Real-time speech-to-sign translation.

Multi-language support (Telugu shown in your interface).

Export transcript for record keeping or learning.

Interactive playback with speed control.

📈 Impact & Applications
Education: Helps teachers communicate with hearing-impaired students.

Public Services: Enables inclusive communication in offices and hospitals.

Learning Tool: Assists beginners in learning sign language interactively.

Accessibility: Promotes social inclusion and awareness.

